  <!-- ZONE 6 — Solomon's Inheritance -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground" data-field="ground" data-tempo="still" data-ground="tinted" data-text="pachinko" data-screen-label="§6 Solomon's Inheritance">
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content">
      <div class="sr-section-opener sr-section-opener--pachinko sr-section-opener--bg-tinted">
        <div class="sr-section-opener-motif" data-motif="medallion-flip" data-size="44"></div>
        <span class="sr-section-opener-eyebrow">The Yonsei</span>
        <h2 class="sr-section-opener-heading">Solomon&rsquo;s Inheritance</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="pk-center pk-center--lead">
        <p>Solomon is the novel&rsquo;s fourth generation &mdash; born in Japan to a wealthy father, schooled internationally, fluent in Japanese and English, educated in America. He has every advantage the earlier generations lacked, and the novel uses him to ask one question: what happens to the family&rsquo;s condition when prosperity, education, and mobility are finally available?</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pk-tracks">
        <div class="pk-track pk-track--before">
          <div class="pk-track-head"><b>Childhood</b> &middot; 1968 &middot; the law</div>
          <blockquote class="pk-track-quote">&ldquo;Let&rsquo;s get your dog tags,&rdquo; Mozasu said.

Solomon faced his father. &ldquo;Hmm?&rdquo;

&ldquo;It&rsquo;s what we dogs must have.&rdquo;<cite>Mozasu, at the ward office</cite></blockquote>
          <div class="pk-track-beat">
            <span class="pk-track-beat-kicker">Registered at fourteen</span>
            <p>Koreans born in Japan after 1952 had to register as foreigners &mdash; reporting to the ward office at fourteen to apply for permission to remain in the only country they had ever known. Lee stages it as a scene: Solomon is photographed and his finger pressed into black ink, depressing it onto the card &ldquo;as if he were a child painting.&rdquo; Mozasu, who has done this himself, looks away from the insulting spectacle.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pk-track-beat">
            <span class="pk-track-beat-kicker">PROCEDURAL CRUELTY</span>
            <p>The violence is bureaucratic: a citizen of nowhere fingerprinted like a criminal as a routine condition of maintaining their ambiguous status. A clerk had once told Mozasu that he was &ldquo;a guest in his country.&rdquo; His conclusion: &ldquo;We can be deported. We have no motherland. Life is full of things he cannot control so he must adapt. My boy has to survive.&rdquo;</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="pk-track pk-track--after">
          <div class="pk-track-head"><b>Adulthood</b> &middot; the bank</div>
          <blockquote class="pk-track-quote">&ldquo;He used your connections, then he fired you by making up some excuse. The client just wanted the Korean shit to go away.&rdquo;<cite>Goro, after the firing</cite></blockquote>
          <div class="pk-track-beat">
            <span class="pk-track-beat-kicker">The cosmopolitan job</span>
            <p>Solomon achieves everything his father planned: a Western investment bank in Tokyo, the kind of institution supposedly free of Japanese prejudice. He is good at it, well liked and seemingly accepted in this world. Then a deal stalls &mdash; an elderly Korean woman will not sell her land &mdash; and the bank discovers that his background, his father&rsquo;s pachinko money and Korean connections, might be useful in moving her.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pk-track-beat">
            <span class="pk-track-beat-kicker">Used, then discarded</span>
            <p>The land is freed; the woman dies of natural causes; and the bank, fearing association with how the obstacle was cleared, fires Solomon to make the problem disappear. His Korean identity was an asset when it was profitable and a liability the moment it was not, a tool whose usefulness had a half-life.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Two Registers diptych -->
      <figure class="pk-registers" aria-label="A diptych: on the left, a fingerprint built from pachinko pins with a gold ball pressed at its core — the registration at fourteen. On the right, a medallion ring expelling that same gold ball off its edge — the firing in adulthood.">
        <span class="pk-registers-eyebrow">The same fact, twice &middot; registered, then discarded</span>
        <div class="pk-registers-pair">
          <div class="pk-register">
            <div class="pk-register-art" data-motif="pk-register" data-side="fingerprint"></div>
            <div class="pk-register-name">The Fingerprint</div>
            <div class="pk-register-sub">age 14 &middot; the ward office</div>
          </div>
          <div class="pk-register-link" aria-hidden="true">
            <span>the same</span>
            <svg width="34" height="14" viewBox="0 0 34 14" fill="none"><circle cx="5" cy="7" r="4" fill="var(--p-gold)"></circle><line x1="11" y1="7" x2="28" y2="7" stroke="var(--p-gold)" stroke-width="1.3"></line><path d="M24 3 L29 7 L24 11" stroke="var(--p-gold)" stroke-width="1.3" fill="none"></path></svg>
            <span>ball</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pk-register">
            <div class="pk-register-art" data-motif="pk-register" data-side="eject"></div>
            <div class="pk-register-name">The Firing</div>
            <div class="pk-register-sub">adulthood &middot; the bank</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="pk-registers-cap">The fingerprint at fourteen and the firing in adulthood are the same inescapable fact in two registers &mdash; belonging that is conditional, revocable, and finally not in Solomon&rsquo;s control.</p>
      </figure>

      <div class="pk-center pk-center--left">
        <p>Noa tried to escape this Zainichi condition by erasing himself, and it destroyed him. Mozasu refused the system&rsquo;s approval and built a life inside the space it left him. Solomon was handed everything those strategies were designed to achieve, only to discover that the condition had only changed costume.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pk-passage pk-passage--mirror">
        <p class="pk-passage-note">Closing the loop<span class="pk-note-sub">defeat, or clarity?</span></p>
        <div class="pk-passage-body">
          <p>Solomon&rsquo;s response reframes the whole novel. After the firing, he chooses to enter his father&rsquo;s business: pachinko, the stigmatized industry Mozasu had hoped to help his son escape. This ending can certainly be read as defeat: the system pushed Solomon back into the margin it reserved for Koreans. Yet it can also be read as a reflection of Mozasu&rsquo;s own clarity, his decision to build on ground he actually owns.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sr-zone-seam" data-from="tinted" data-to="cream" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-half sr-zone-seam-half--from"></div>
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-motif" data-motif="rosette" data-size="44"></div>
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-half sr-zone-seam-half--to"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- ZONE 7 — Kyunghee Is Waiting -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground" data-field="ground" data-tempo="still" data-ground="cream" data-text="pachinko" data-screen-label="§7 Someone Is Waiting">
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content">
      <div class="sr-section-opener sr-section-opener--pachinko sr-section-opener--bg-cream">
        <div class="sr-section-opener-motif" data-motif="medallion-flip" data-size="44"></div>
        <span class="sr-section-opener-eyebrow">The Final Image</span>
        <h2 class="sr-section-opener-heading">Kyunghee Is Waiting</h2>
      </div>

      <figure class="sr-pullquote sr-pullquote--pachinko pk-zone-pull">
        <div class="sr-pullquote-bracket sr-pullquote-bracket--top" data-motif="floral" data-w="120"></div>
        <blockquote class="sr-pullquote-body"><p>&ldquo;Japan will never change. It will never ever integrate gaijin, and my darling, here you will always be a gaijin and never Japanese.&rdquo;</p></blockquote>
        <figcaption class="sr-pullquote-attribution">&mdash; <em>Pachinko</em>, Min Jin Lee (Hana to Solomon)</figcaption>
        <div class="sr-pullquote-bracket sr-pullquote-bracket--bottom" data-motif="floral" data-w="120"></div>
      </figure>

      <div class="pk-passage">
        <p class="pk-passage-note">No escape offered<span class="pk-note-sub">and yet not despair</span></p>
        <div class="pk-passage-body pk-passage-body--lede">
          <p>Across four generations, the novel has refused to offer escape and resolution for the dilemmas faced by Sunja and her family. Hana&rsquo;s warning to Solomon is the bleak distillation of everything the story has traced. Yet the novel does not end on a note of despair.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pk-passage pk-passage--mirror">
        <p class="pk-passage-note">The final image<span class="pk-note-sub">neither nation; a person</span></p>
        <div class="pk-passage-body">
          <p>The novel&rsquo;s final image is the closest thing to an answer Lee is willing to provide. Sunja, now old, visits Noa&rsquo;s grave. She buries a photograph in the earth, covers the hole, picks up her bags, and heads back to where Kyunghee is waiting for her. Home, in this image, is not Korea or Japan. It is neither of the nations that failed the family. The home that Sunja has earned through her struggle is the specific person waiting at the place where she lives &mdash; the particular relationship that has outlasted all of the failures of history.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="pk-figure" aria-label="Someone Waiting — a journey leaving a buried photograph, passing between two un-entered nation rings, arriving at a waiting figure.">
        <span class="pk-figure-eyebrow">Someone waiting &middot; home is a person</span>
        <div class="pk-figure-art" data-motif="pk-figure" data-fig="waiting"></div>
        <p class="pk-figure-cap">Sunja&rsquo;s journey to the cemetery leaves a photograph buried at a grave and passes between the two nations &mdash; entering neither.</p>
      </figure>

      <div class="pk-center pk-center--coda">
        <p>&ldquo;Kyunghee would be waiting for her at home.&rdquo; The novel&rsquo;s last sentence echoes its first &mdash; &ldquo;History has failed us, but no matter.&rdquo; The &ldquo;no matter&rdquo; that the book earns, across eighty years and four generations, is this: history has taken the nation, the name, the legal standing, the promised reward from the Zainichi. It cannot take away the life that Sunja has built for herself and her family.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="pk-end">
    <div class="sr-end-signature sr-end-signature--pachinko" data-motif="butterfly" data-size="72" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  </div>

  <SpokePagination
    section="pachinko"
    prev={{ slug: 'figures', title: 'Key Figures', number: 4 }}
  />
</SummerLayout>

<script>
(function () {
  'use strict';

  /* ── Motif generators ── */
  function rosette(size: number, color: string, spokes: number): string {
    var c = size / 2, ro = size * 0.46, ri = size * 0.10, dr = size * 0.05, lines = '';
    for (var i = 0; i < spokes; i++) {
      var a = i * 2 * Math.PI / spokes;
      lines += '<line x1="' + (c + Math.cos(a) * ri) + '" y1="' + (c + Math.sin(a) * ri) +
        '" x2="' + (c + Math.cos(a) * ro) + '" y2="' + (c + Math.sin(a) * ro) +
        '" stroke="' + color + '" stroke-width="1" stroke-linecap="round"/>';
    }
    return '<svg width="' + size + '" height="' + size + '" viewBox="0 0 ' + size + ' ' + size + '" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">' +
      lines + '<circle cx="' + c + '" cy="' + c + '" r="' + dr + '" fill="var(--p-gold)"/></svg>';
  }

  function medallionFlip(size: number): string {
    var c = size / 2, ro = size * 0.44, ri = size * 0.30, r2 = size * 0.20, dot = size * 0.045;
